WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.430
Hello everyone, today I'm in Chicago

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.810
and I presented my work at the Supercomputing Conference

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:12.910
about enabling radio astronomy to use French supercomputers.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.590
So, next generation radio telescopes such as the SKA

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:22.310
will produce exascale volumes of data

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:26.950
that needs national HPC centres to process it.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.640
And astrophysicians [sic] traditionally use

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.740
local clusters with shared access models.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.720
So they have one account for multiple users.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.500
And at the other hand [sic], French supercomputers

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.180
are classified as strategic national assets.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.200
And the cybersecurity regulations

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.540
imposed one account per person.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.940
So we move today this friction

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.080
by exposing scientific applications as a service.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.620
So this is the next fix for astronomy.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.300
And today, astronomers from South Africa,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.500
England and France can connect through our website,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.440
select the scientific application they want to use

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.760
to study galaxies, pulsars, black holes.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.380
They can tweak applicative parameters and hit on play and that's it.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.580
They don't have to worry about HPC complexity.

